# A fenced code block draws its own surface, and does not punch through the bubble behind it.
#
# The fence used to fill with theme::window_bg(). Inside an assistant bubble,
# which fills with something else, that is a hole clean through the bubble: the
# reader sees one bubble, a gap, and another bubble. It survived because the
# parity metric could not see it -- window_bg (23,23,35) is within 12 of the
# bubble's (33,33,54), so a full-width rectangle of the wrong colour scored as
# a match.
#
# The reference draws (19,19,27), a step DARKER than the bubble rather than a
# step lighter, and it draws it per LINE: measured on ref/02_thread.png, the
# long error line's chip runs x384..1018 and the "exit 65" line's runs
# x374..435, in the same block.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1180,"window_height":949,"open_tabs":["r9"],"active_tab":"r9","pinned_tabs":[],"theme":"dark"}

# The block itself paints nothing -- it is a column of chips, not a panel. A
# zero-height assertion would be ambiguous with "not rendered", so this asserts
# the two things a panel would contradict: the block is as wide as its parent
# and its LINES are not.
assert_ui code_block hidden=false

# The two lines of ref/02_thread.png's fence, each sized to its own text. The
# long one is six times the short one -- which is the whole claim, and what a
# single panel makes impossible: under a panel both are the block's width.
assert_ui_text "exit 65" w=42
assert_ui_text "error: no signing identity matched 'fbmacos-apps-inhouse'" w=276

# NOT asserted with expect_text: a STYLED label is invisible to it. Both of
# these lines carry syntax runs, and the renderer draws the runs INSTEAD of
# the label, so the plain-text index `expect_text` searches never sees them --
# it reports the sidebar's rows and nothing from the transcript. The same
# blind spot as gap #61 one layer over: the harness can read what a widget
# was told to say, and not what it drew.
